  "step_2": "The individual first considers Lottery B because it has only one option (100% probability of 1500 Euros). The individual then evaluates the expected value: 1500 Euros with 100% probability, which is straightforward. Based on this, the individual concludes that Lottery A is not worth considering and directly chooses Lottery B. There is no mention of probabilities of loss, risk, skewness, or any consideration of unfavorable outcomes. The deliberation focuses solely on expected value and simplicity (only one outcome), not on the distribution shape or skewness of outcomes. The individual does not discuss the possibility of losing money or the balance between gains and losses in Lottery A. Therefore, the reported reasoning does not involve any evaluation of skewness or the proportion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — skewness — is not considered in the verbal report. The individual does not mention unfavorable outcomes, probabilities of loss, or the shape of the distribution. The reported reasoning is based solely on expected value and simplicity, not on skewness. The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery has a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes (i.e., right-skewness), but this is absent from the individual’s thought process. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The justification provided is unrelated to skewness and instead centers on expected value and outcome certainty. Since the individual completely ignores the concept of skewness and does not apply the rule of preferring the more right-skewed lottery, the reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
